{:edb/db-before-t 2414 :edb/db-after-t 2415 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535727 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:02:47.796Z" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221110 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139535727 true] [17592186221110 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 6y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5$, with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$, respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139535727 false] [17592186221110 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/cfff34f683e4c790f0f669a1babde2e9da9fcc10769c674f037ed3328d658f9f.png)\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/75c8006b52acb68b62c4f827fdd7b8c08709c647c7994f3088bf4f8f72eb3b8f.png) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/236a2688de746c23166d9159dfc073781ed55bc33cb289230f8bc9dbc00e323d.png) has the equation \n$y=- \\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/2527c2ffbb46a109eac9386b6b4bb634ca98768cf261252a2b94daf72f11a4a0.png)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221110 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}$. These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/04a7ef2e0aa4f7e78b2e2d6bcd3ecc67.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-\\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/29bacb4b190583c38b6f6c1e63da2895.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$. So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/c788bfa30548a6e1dcaf080f3d6fcaf0.png)" 13194139535727 false] [17592186221111 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139535727 true] [17592186221111 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) + 9y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T$. The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}$. Sketch the phase portrait of the system." 13194139535727 false] [17592186221111 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3t - 1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThese solutions form the line with the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac13x\n$$\n\nin the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/1d5dfee4e3ecc9de1abd7b5c988c3572.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n${x = 3t + 2; y = t + 1$\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/b2eab0b163b394484f17ad33df6c1d82.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1$, we get the following curve:\n${x =-3t - 2; y =-t - 1$\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/7555ac8c4917b8f00d3dddd94a3a9e7c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/9c4709214d5c0674d33a227c8b36885c.png)" 13194139535727 false] [17592186221111 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/32cdfec727ba37d7c21b4df53d40d29853c56a0bf7c5469227882de8bceb735a.png) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/810d48ff45c63e14ede1c9b7742298b263c861bba689595af6fe7afeac69f96d.png) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/93ec8854dd09842895e8f0854683a03c2cae129198802f9b49c3d426179eb78b.png)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221112 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." 13194139535727 true] [17592186221112 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) + 5y(t)$\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." 13194139535727 false] [17592186221112 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1e5a8eef63597c74566c624df6f52cf446c13d6d0ae16612b5ac3c45479f8816.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/9169c7aaf58e872a751777fffb26d435573ece6d79b1af64ccbe4b1bf6544463.png)\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y=0 \\\\ 5x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n2y=-2x\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/72ee846ffe127bc7d4fb9e7b37b633f2c07e71f439c18471792e05fb56d0f7e1.png)\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x.$" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221112 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2x + 2y = 0; 5x + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2y=-2x \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y=-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x$." 13194139535727 false] [17592186221345 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363439 13194139535727 true] [17592186221345 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363446 13194139535727 true] [17592186221345 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363453 13194139535727 true] [17592186221345 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363460 13194139535727 true] [17592186221345 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363467 13194139535727 true] [17592186221346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363474 13194139535727 true] [17592186221346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363481 13194139535727 true] [17592186221346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363488 13194139535727 true] [17592186221347 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363495 13194139535727 true] [17592186221347 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363502 13194139535727 true] [17592186221347 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363509 13194139535727 true] [17592186221347 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363516 13194139535727 true] [17592186221347 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363523 13194139535727 true] [17592186221887 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186221887 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221887 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} = 0$ and $λ_{2} = 3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [1, 2]^{T}$, respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 false] [17592186221887 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221933 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186221933 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186221933 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} = 0$ and $λ_{2} =-3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [1, 2]^{T}$, respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 false] [17592186221933 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/3bc608bdc2cfd22f6fad528c0b2d3cd421355e4bef810a862b4585a2f85409a3.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363440 13194139535727 true] [17592186363439 1089 "q-333782" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363439 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363439 1113 #uuid "f221dffc-2120-578b-a4ad-5bc088d9fd5e" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363439 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363441 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363442 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363443 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363444 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363445 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363445 13194139535727 true] [17592186363440 1110 #uuid "bc076757-718a-5547-94b4-ed52aeb3633c" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363441 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363441 1111 #uuid "8ca89864-12cd-52a7-9885-0d6ef492b0c8" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363442 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363442 1111 #uuid "3fbdeedd-d07a-5db3-98e9-435ad492b799" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363443 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363443 1111 #uuid "e153a55b-0b7a-5795-8a85-025bef005b5e" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363444 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363444 1111 #uuid "4c00f3ae-05f4-5f30-90e2-8c9045416388" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363445 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363445 1111 #uuid "b63852cf-c846-525a-bfcc-3f89bc301f57" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363446 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363447 13194139535727 true] [17592186363446 1089 "q-333793" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363446 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363446 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363446 1113 #uuid "99732f9a-ac2c-5636-946a-f27a7124f0f2" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363446 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=0 \\\\ 6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-3x.$" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363448 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363449 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363451 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363449 13194139535727 true] [17592186363447 1110 #uuid "42d68b84-ec28-59a4-9557-f44c325080db" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363448 1034 "y=3x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363448 1111 #uuid "ed7456c2-2678-52e7-820a-99bc15ccaf4a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363449 1034 "y=-3x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363449 1111 #uuid "980a8502-8d4a-5f2c-85c1-56ba245e2e24" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363450 1034 "y=x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363450 1111 #uuid "3580e341-d32f-54c8-89be-15651feba42f" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363451 1034 "y=-3x+1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363451 1111 #uuid "a9f043dc-50f7-5f0a-a447-ffca5c9519d8" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363452 1034 "y=-x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363452 1111 #uuid "112a9aaf-98d4-5911-9433-bf809bbe4af1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363454 13194139535727 true] [17592186363453 1089 "q-333794" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363453 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363453 1113 #uuid "ebbc730a-95c5-5beb-a9ec-077ed11a8ad1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363453 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363455 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363456 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363457 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363458 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363459 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363457 13194139535727 true] [17592186363454 1110 #uuid "e75280c8-9a38-538b-99fe-131d59035955" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363455 1034 "y=-\\frac{x}{2}" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363455 1111 #uuid "babe4169-da41-5dfb-8525-b93df353477a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363456 1034 "y=2x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363456 1111 #uuid "d2d769db-9054-5f8c-9c94-be8ff623d02e" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363457 1034 "y=\\frac{x}{2}" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363457 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363457 1111 #uuid "c3eeda67-4f3e-5d93-8249-9fb2b358efda" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363458 1034 "y=\\frac{x}{2}+1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363458 1111 #uuid "457043e1-e842-57b2-abb6-ccbb5c5163b8" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363459 1034 "y=-2x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363459 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363459 1111 #uuid "6b243375-e114-5727-b300-1d1a336b8c4b" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363460 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363461 13194139535727 true] [17592186363460 1089 "q-333795" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363460 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=8x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363460 1113 #uuid "834e558d-96b8-5e1a-8566-5efdab5d5716" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363460 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363462 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363464 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363465 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363466 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363462 13194139535727 true] [17592186363461 1110 #uuid "a34bba82-691a-51e5-8b75-bec388cb24ab" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363462 1034 "y=2x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363462 1111 #uuid "a43efbaa-312c-5cd9-a8e4-f843478df1b6" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363463 1034 "y=x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363463 1111 #uuid "b702c003-1dff-5caa-af31-e9afc75eead6" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363464 1034 "y=2x-1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363464 1111 #uuid "2e59727e-378d-50ae-94c2-3b4d613e7294" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363465 1034 "y=-2x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363465 1111 #uuid "b21e0c86-7dfc-565e-bcb5-c1b099f51474" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363466 1034 "y=-x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363466 1111 #uuid "81a67f2f-4d21-50ea-89b1-1f744b0f3e1a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363467 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363468 13194139535727 true] [17592186363467 1089 "q-333800" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363467 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363467 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363467 1113 #uuid "a0fb8460-6d29-597b-a1bc-86d67ad6b10f" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363467 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=-x\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{x}{2}.$" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363469 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363470 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363471 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363472 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363473 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363470 13194139535727 true] [17592186363468 1110 #uuid "a7a966f6-5de1-5b20-b4ab-e1b5c7effe97" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363469 1034 "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363469 1111 #uuid "aa92a68e-70ae-5f8e-813b-056fa20b6f3b" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363470 1034 "y=-\\frac{x}{2}" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363470 1111 #uuid "f4d458e7-4384-531c-aec0-93aa0a63cae7" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363471 1034 "y=2x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363471 1111 #uuid "e6255126-f3cf-5792-a6a8-fbffa6f46190" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363472 1034 "y=\\frac{x}{2}" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363472 1111 #uuid "9f19094e-d72b-5d7b-b028-3e412ec048b0" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363473 1034 "y=-2x" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535727 true] [17592186363473 1111 #uuid "b795e39b-2c69-533b-87a8-8b200d73715c" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363474 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363475 13194139535727 true] [17592186363474 1089 "q-333814" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363474 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363474 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363474 1113 #uuid "c13a40a3-bc1a-5171-a2d7-22e438148666" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363474 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363476 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363477 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363478 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363479 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363480 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363480 13194139535727 true] [17592186363475 1110 #uuid "e5bcda7d-f3af-541a-bcb1-7a6771f61fb4" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363476 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/f7784ccd8bbfe05ce743e71f92cfbecfa4d4a8b19d0664337d2c67d360b3d3e8.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363476 1111 #uuid "1c0507b2-ba04-549b-8f7f-8bd0e44e970b" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363477 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/a76320ebfd18b1bc272c5421309a4a98c34abc5437c56c6d40a83a84f80f0157.png" 1319413