um solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363481 1113 #uuid "51efb4d6-e9f7-5bc8-9cf9-600d5ab52ec8" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363481 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/41fff6a9cacaf140a6f86b498ba9415aef0a4699fcb119146ee49e0c98843eeb.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/3499c19ed90a1cbd1916878fe2b9b26401c4eedd656cb9026fbdf35e5b78acb4.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363483 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363484 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363485 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363486 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363487 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363487 13194139535727 true] [17592186363482 1110 #uuid "afd9949d-d8c8-5ad4-ae58-78a94157774a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363483 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/475a2dc2e5e9301afeb1e9d123568912a98469514c4385f765ecb41f29c8c430.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363483 1111 #uuid "243d8ed5-06fa-5ce5-8c3b-ef8c09f6afc1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363484 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/6902407f64e3b547e7c5cabf53c762b16997e9528d88acc315ca78e8e9187b96.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363484 1111 #uuid "65acea10-b161-549d-8bbe-763454bd15ae" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363485 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/5e1bb1629b32dea80f057fff4cf447f39cd4f44cc379337237d0f929da809f2d.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363485 1111 #uuid "762e23b8-09e0-5ae8-9737-b2a7cf290c07" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363486 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/5c77066f6b59335facea48ea56bf9256044775768d3dddcb064335a1aa7653f8.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363486 1111 #uuid "e4d4488f-dfe0-5a66-a4cd-22ac616109d9" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363487 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363487 1111 #uuid "2f49ea8a-3b91-56c5-ac0e-9075ab539fcb" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363489 13194139535727 true] [17592186363488 1089 "q-334106" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363488 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363488 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363488 1113 #uuid "f1d32c3a-674d-5f6b-9af5-31eda216d3b2" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363488 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/40b02cfc27ab7bc7bcc4a76523e106066b6ed6f56c184c7bd1f59a0cd1dcfc27.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363490 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363491 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363492 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363493 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363494 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363494 13194139535727 true] [17592186363489 1110 #uuid "dc53dd9a-307c-52aa-82c4-6855c918d714" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363490 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/f4abb32b9a492e7d39b4504af7cf2a180e74dcca679804ed8198089cb68b7fb6.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363490 1111 #uuid "ecdbc8f4-9acf-59c7-b9cf-29c23bae3f98" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363491 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/cee58e76fac807cd504037385e0b7c0b8aed1f726a3c6e010d127100d7274004.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363491 1111 #uuid "a99b20a6-839d-5d61-a35d-5eab66fbb0ac" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363492 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/87e39cbca1f6b5179f09a1526cf326de4ed205d9df5d0f86ee68585780e16a5f.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363492 1111 #uuid "b426c0d3-8ef6-57f8-9110-ce1263157e4f" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363493 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/63422efd3b1af838ee4bf6a7ac87e39694c2ed5e7b6931d16c70773f688abfbb.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363493 1111 #uuid "c3478cc6-892b-5b34-a6e1-b0d306416da6" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363494 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363494 1111 #uuid "bfa334b2-1346-5f69-88bd-8f226a049fff" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363495 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363496 13194139535727 true] [17592186363495 1089 "q-334532" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363495 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363495 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363495 1113 #uuid "8cb0146a-edb8-5224-b29c-36d6ad8aec81" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363495 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/5c3afc4bedb411c778773190a3b9bc1db0b376dd69592ba744e8225ec900cc45.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-1 \\\\ y=2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/0d506993d3bfbb727dffd44ced1a588b40e2fefa86e7f2780d90277c9d0a9eed.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=-2t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/e88dfa02c57a5c31df14fdf6f963f7d568eaebccbbbfba6d8f2958593ac4693a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363497 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363498 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363499 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363500 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363501 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363499 13194139535727 true] [17592186363496 1110 #uuid "13868889-8188-5d77-9d87-826a0ea799ea" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363497 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/879cc00fedd506a0607bce3d3d36f99b489f599dadb6b13d4d1176fa24441853.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363497 1111 #uuid "307946bb-8890-592c-bdaa-745bcf295cf0" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363498 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/c9a56a6a1f17d58226fb2fac905247b339be111335637a15d3e8c447aab3f3a8.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363498 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363498 1111 #uuid "f8620cc7-ed77-5736-8d4c-3b8242117fa9" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363499 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363499 1111 #uuid "f342fd00-b364-5290-8091-aaef49990118" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363500 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363500 1111 #uuid "a941a641-a751-5161-bb94-a1505a69cac5" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363501 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1dca375ebe6f8468f12a1729d697d8d35ff6b430a70e53639ab029254ac392fa.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363501 1111 #uuid "8ed6b293-d4ad-5fc8-886b-5b5e5e6b5ed9" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363502 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363503 13194139535727 true] [17592186363502 1089 "q-334620" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363502 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363502 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363502 1113 #uuid "64b317e4-a8a4-59a8-823b-ca9d893b0330" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363502 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+1 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/f7be54fdf4b1cbe65be9730989c06b934604bc8f0b4300bd6b616ece9de7b8f5.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-1 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/47c41f2d62aea217d56259bf9918b1eba5bed3141d2bcfcfd486241bc32df048.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363504 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363505 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363506 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363507 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363508 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363506 13194139535727 true] [17592186363503 1110 #uuid "9a0f756d-8653-5ebe-a90d-c6efc3daa310" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363504 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363504 1111 #uuid "0537f49d-2dc1-5c72-92a2-ef58592c4112" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363505 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/c999c1938803ff00ff445cdeb063f679e06acf47109cbcba01eeb6ed502e3d33.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363505 1111 #uuid "4367c2e3-1041-542a-b512-ada746faeadb" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363506 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363506 1111 #uuid "bd6c22af-b92a-564a-a9a5-7165bb49948a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363507 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363507 1111 #uuid "caf0f08b-ede6-5bb0-b3e9-2047b50c7c9a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363508 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363508 1111 #uuid "94fef23d-d374-5462-bde1-5bf32434bf42" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363509 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363510 13194139535727 true] [17592186363509 1089 "q-334624" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363509 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363509 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363509 1113 #uuid "7647edfd-c6e3-5d84-bb00-419fbe5a4b5a" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363509 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/58564c40fcde276fb69732ce5926b7e350fe8963b51b21df0ce4de39e8481514.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363511 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363512 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363513 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363514 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363515 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363511 13194139535727 true] [17592186363510 1110 #uuid "87845eb3-3546-5109-9b6a-bb4613ab13b9" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363511 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363511 1111 #uuid "5defdbe6-b911-5a80-a158-2a12b1140f18" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363512 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/b47e03cb4429684acceac875e9c42875af53e0fe362125d2527bb62a24da1143.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363512 1111 #uuid "daa6e9f9-3099-5538-ae72-2ddb20e04623" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363513 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363513 1111 #uuid "e506460e-cb3e-53e8-94ef-2ca4b9cb85e9" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363514 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bb3187e3229b7fc09f530035d1dffe0e8690e8056502ecb27fcb41c274b6b218.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363514 1111 #uuid "5cf6c5d2-2a92-5b4a-8b22-f7a8763b1358" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363515 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/fe4863ed53356be2d5a2995d72372271544f55a86846bd0179dd82cf04844f58.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363515 1111 #uuid "f07b6939-473a-54ef-b6ca-1beb2fa9bd24" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363516 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363517 13194139535727 true] [17592186363516 1089 "q-334639" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363516 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363516 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363516 1113 #uuid "c07d6a46-4ba6-5448-9f13-79808c35273d" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363516 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/6044d391016e37af929b36e89e0a802151b5710707cc510e9e7f70bf9a2d1c0d.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-2 \\\\ y=2t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363518 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363519 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363520 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363521 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363522 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1041 "selection" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363519 13194139535727 true] [17592186363517 1110 #uuid "a10e3bd2-3da2-50f8-b625-bbcfd33739be" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363518 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/c9a56a6a1f17d58226fb2fac905247b339be111335637a15d3e8c447aab3f3a8.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363518 1111 #uuid "2e16bca0-b3d6-546b-9705-ef3b59f708d1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363519 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363519 1111 #uuid "96ff6596-227d-5493-a614-91d4a7441689" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363520 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/41a70a3485c8b5000c832ac5a9d443c8abb576d6d60cb0391b2cc4cf2cae60c0.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363520 1111 #uuid "9bda4691-1f3a-5ccf-adf3-1f844b8da3cb" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363521 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1dca375ebe6f8468f12a1729d697d8d35ff6b430a70e53639ab029254ac392fa.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363521 1111 #uuid "d3d27b89-bbaa-5ff6-936d-d14020cd010b" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363522 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535727 true] [17592186363522 1111 #uuid "e17b5a2e-0fa8-548a-822f-b2bbd03576f1" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363523 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363524 13194139535727 true] [17592186363523 1089 "q-334641" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363523 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535727 true] [17592186363523 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363523 1113 #uuid "4dc3f93b-be82-5bba-ac8d-804adc4e23df" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363523 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+3 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4t-3 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)" 13194139535727 true] [17592186363524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363525 13194139535